How I Troubleshoot Kubernetes Issues (Step-by-Step Approach)

After facing multiple real-time Kubernetes issues, I learned that a structured troubleshooting approach saves hours of time.

Here’s how I debug Kubernetes issues in real environments:

 Step 1: Check Pod Status
kubectl get pods
Identifies issues like CrashLoopBackOff, Pending, ImagePullBackOff

 Step 2: Describe the Pod
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
Events section often reveals the actual root cause
Useful for scheduling, image, and resource issues


 Step 3: Check Application Logs
kubectl logs <pod-name>
Confirms whether the issue is application-level or platform-level
For multi-container pods:
kubectl logs <pod> -c <container>


 Step 4: Validate Configurations
Environment variables
ConfigMaps & Secrets
Ports & probes (liveness/readiness)


 Step 5: Verify Services & Networking
kubectl get svc
kubectl describe svc <service-name>
Ensures labels, selectors, and ports match correctly


Step 6: Check Node & Resource Status
kubectl get nodes
kubectl describe node <node>
Helps identify CPU/memory pressure and taints



Most Kubernetes issues can be solved by reading events and logs carefully before changing anything.
